    12	# Design analysis for the fused variant:
    13	# - Concrete behavior: each 4x4 frame still wraps a 2x2 monochrome block, but some border-wall
    14	#   pixels are recolored into a single marker color. The count of those marker pixels is the
    15	#   collision priority for all beams emitted by that frame. Example: a frame with 3 markers and
    16	#   one east-facing gap meets a frame with 1 marker and one north-facing gap. In the original
    17	#   task both beams would redirect symmetrically; here the 3-marker beam keeps going while the
    18	#   1-marker beam alone bends.
    19	# - Complexity calibration: priorities are limited to 1/2/3 markers so humans can count them
    20	#   quickly, and all markers share one color so the input does not hide a second legend in hue.
    21	#   Generator samples distinct priorities for the beams that actually collide, preventing the
    22	#   variant from collapsing back to the symmetric original.
    23	# - Broken assumptions from the base task:
    24	#   1. Border pixels are no longer a single frame color, so frame inference must identify the
    25	#      dominant wall color and treat other non-bg/non-block border colors as markers.
    26	#   2. Structural-gap checks can no longer require exact frame-color neighbors; they must accept
    27	#      either frame-color or marker-color walls.
    28	#   3. Collision code can no longer assume both participants change state together. The winner
    29	#      keeps its path, while only the lower-priority beam is repositioned and retargeted.
    30	# - Placement legality: the generator uses explicit 90-degree or 45-degree collision templates,
    31	#   keeps frame bounding boxes separated, keeps every frame inside the grid, and validates the
    32	#   finished grid by rerunning the solver. Extra randomness only affects marker placement, colors,
    33	#   distances, and which beam wins.
    34	# - Visual observability and cleanliness: marker count is a visible, local cue on each frame;
    35	#   the winning ray is the one whose marker count is larger. Output remains legible because each
    36	#   training-style sample has a single intended priority clash and uses one gap per frame, so the
    37	#   asymmetric bend is easy to isolate with the eye.
